---
import BaseLayout from "../layouts/BaseLayout.astro";
import CodeBlock from "../components/CodeBlock.astro";
import { release } from "../data/release";

const bootstrap = release.installCommand;
const windows = release.windowsInstallCommand;
const installerUpgrade = release.installerUpgradeCommand;
const upgrade = release.upgradeCommand;

const up = `curl -fsSL ${release.installScriptUrl} | sudo sh   # apt repo + storm-server only
sudo storm-server up
sudo storm-server status`;

const upCustom = `sudo storm-server up \\
  --data-root /srv/storm \\
  --vault-root /path/to/vaults \\
  --state /srv/storm/state`;

const pair = `sudo -u storm storm-server pair \\
  --state /srv/storm/state --qr`;

const passwd = `sudo -u storm storm-server passwd --state /srv/storm/state`;
---

<BaseLayout
  title="Install"
  description="Install Storm on Linux, macOS or Windows with the interactive installer, then pair your devices and download the apps."
>
  <header class="page-hero wrap-narrow">
    <p class="eyebrow">08 · Installation</p>
    <h1>Install Storm</h1>
    <p class="lede">
      One command on Linux (Debian/Ubuntu) or macOS opens an interactive
      installer: the server and app, a Runtime Host for agents, or everything,
      with your phone paired and the runtime enrolled for you. Windows has its
      own. Each shows its plan before changing anything; run it again to
      upgrade, pair a device or uninstall. Then
      <a href="/clients">get a client</a>
      for your other devices.
    </p>
  </header>

  <section class="section wrap-narrow" id="linux">
    <h2>Linux and macOS</h2>
    <p class="support">
      On Linux the installer uses Storm’s signed apt repository; on macOS it
      downloads the release, checks every file against its checksums, and runs
      the server as you with your notes in <code>~/Storm</code>. Served from
      the apt repo root at
      <a href={release.aptRoot} target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer"
        >dewanshdt.github.io/Storm</a
      >
      — that URL is the apt root, not the marketing site at storm.dewansh.space.
    </p>
    <CodeBlock code={bootstrap} label="install" />
  </section>

  <section class="section wrap-narrow" id="windows">
    <h2>Windows</h2>
    <p class="support">
      In PowerShell — as administrator if you want the Runtime Host. It
      installs the server, the Runtime Host, or both, after checking every
      download against the release’s checksums. The server runs as you at
      logon with no console window and your notes in
      <code>%USERPROFILE%\Storm</code>; the Runtime Host is the
      <code>StormRuntime</code> service under its own virtual account. Neither
      is code-signed yet, so SmartScreen may warn.
    </p>
    <CodeBlock code={windows} label="powershell" />
  </section>

  <section class="section wrap-narrow" id="update">
    <h2>Update</h2>
    <p class="support">
      Run the installer again: it shows what is installed and offers to
      upgrade it, and it never deletes notes or workspaces. On Windows, re-run
      the PowerShell command above. If you run both the server and a Runtime
      Host, upgrade both — newer features need both sides.
    </p>
    <CodeBlock code={installerUpgrade} label="update" />
    <p class="support">
      A server installed by hand on Linux upgrades through apt — there is no
      <code>storm-server upgrade</code>. That refreshes the binary and the web
      client under <code>/usr/share/storm/web</code>. Restart the unit so the
      new binary is what is listening; <code>/etc/storm/storm.env</code> is left
      alone across upgrades.
    </p>
    <CodeBlock code={upgrade} label="update · apt" />
  </section>

  <section class="section wrap-narrow" id="start">
    <h2>Start the server by hand</h2>
    <p class="support">
      The installer does this for you. By hand on Debian or Ubuntu, register
      the apt repository and start the server: <code>up</code> creates the <code>storm</code> user when it can, writes
      <code>/etc/storm/storm.env</code> (mode 600), and enables the systemd
      unit. Default data root is <code>/srv/storm</code>. The web client is then
      at <code>http://&lt;host&gt;:{release.webPort}</code>.
    </p>
    <p class="support">
      <strong>There is no token in that file.</strong> Storm has no shared
      password and no cloud account — credentials are per device and live in
      <code>state/auth.db</code>, never in a config file. Getting the first
      device in is the next step.
    </p>
    <CodeBlock code={up} label="up" />
  </section>

  <section class="section wrap-narrow" id="first-login">
    <h2>First login</h2>
    <p class="support">
      Storm is single-user: one account, with a password you set when the
      first device pairs. Everything after that — more devices, MCP keys,
      Runtime Hosts — is added from a client that is already signed in, under
      <strong>Settings</strong>.
    </p>

    <h3>From a browser</h3>
    <p class="support">
      Open <code>http://&lt;host&gt;:{release.webPort}</code> on the same
      network. The page pairs itself with the server that served it, then asks
      you to set your Storm’s password. Nothing to run on the host.
    </p>

    <h3>From the macOS or Android app</h3>
    <p class="support">
      On your home network the app lists the Storms it finds under
      <strong>On this network</strong>. Pick yours and enter your Storm’s
      password. On a network your computer treats as public, or when the app
      cannot see the server, scan a QR code instead: <strong>Settings ›
      Devices &amp; access › Add a device</strong> on a device that is already
      signed in shows one.
    </p>
    <p class="support">
      The very first device has no password to enter yet. Set it up from the
      browser, or print a single-use setup code on the host and scan it.
      <code>pair</code> works only while the server has no account.
    </p>
    <CodeBlock code={pair} label="pair" />

    <h3>On the host</h3>
    <p class="support">
      A forgotten password is reset on the box, and on a server with no
      account yet the same command creates it. <strong>Run it as
      <code>storm</code>, not as root</strong> — <code>auth.db</code> is created on first use, so a
      root-owned database is one the service cannot write, and the symptom
      arrives much later as a login that fails with nothing useful in the
      journal.
    </p>
    <CodeBlock code={passwd} label="passwd" />
  </section>

  <section class="section wrap-narrow" id="firewalls">
    <h2>Firewalls</h2>
    <p class="support">
      Storm needs no inbound port from the internet and never opens one
      silently. On your home network it is faster and more private to let
      devices reach Storm directly, so the installer <strong>asks</strong>
      first, and only ever allows Storm’s port from your local network: ufw
      rules from your local subnets, firewalld’s home zone (never public),
      Windows’ <strong>Private</strong> networks only, and macOS’s own
      <em>Allow incoming connections?</em> prompt. Say no and it prints the
      command instead; uninstalling removes only what it added.
    </p>
    <p class="support">
      On a network the computer treats as public, such as a café or hotel,
      Storm does not announce itself and will not pair by password; scan a QR
      code instead. <code>storm-server status</code> says whether the firewall
      lets Storm in, and prints the command to fix it if not.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="section wrap-narrow">
    <h2>Custom paths</h2>
    <p class="support">
      Point <code>--vault-root</code> at a directory that <em>contains</em>
      vaults (one folder per vault), not at a single vault. Vaults on NFS often
      need the process to run as a user that can write the mount —
      <code>up</code> follows the state-dir owner in that case.
    </p>
    <CodeBlock code={upCustom} label="up · custom" />
  </section>

  <section class="section wrap-narrow" id="clients">
    <h2>Clients</h2>
    <p class="support">
      Native builds for macOS and Android, plus the web UI served by your
      server. Downloads and checksums live on the
      <a href="/clients">Clients</a> page.
    </p>
    <p class="cta-row">
      <a class="btn btn-primary" href="/clients">Get a client →</a>
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="section wrap-narrow">
    <h2>MCP</h2>
    <p class="support">
      After the server is up, turn MCP on in the app under
      <strong>Settings › AI access</strong> — it is off by default, and the
      write tools need <em>Let them create, edit and delete</em>. An MCP client
      authenticates with a <em>key</em> you mint under Devices &amp; access,
      shown once and revocable; it is accepted on <code>/mcp</code> and nowhere
      else.
    </p>
    <p class="support">
      Making a key shows the config for your AI app, filled in for your
      server. The recommended one runs <code>storm-runtime mcp-connect</code>,
      a small adapter the app starts itself: it checks it reached your server
      before sending the key, and if your server uses a relay the same config
      keeps working away from home. It needs <code>storm-runtime</code> on that computer. The plain
      HTTP entry also works, on your home network only.
    </p>
    <p class="support">
      The tools are listed on the <a href="/#mcp">home page</a> and
      documented in
      <a
        href="https://github.com/dewanshDT/Storm/blob/main/apps/server/README.md"
        target="_blank"
        rel="noopener noreferrer">apps/server/README.md</a
      >.
    </p>
  </section>
</BaseLayout>

<style>
  code {
    font-family: var(--font-mono);
    font-size: 0.92em;
    color: var(--text);
  }

  .section h3 {
    margin: calc(var(--sp) * 5) 0 calc(var(--sp) * 1.5);
    font-family: var(--font-sans);
    font-size: var(--fs-md);
    font-weight: 600;
    color: var(--text);
  }

  .section h3:first-of-type {
    margin-top: calc(var(--sp) * 2);
  }

  .support em {
    font-style: italic;
    color: var(--text);
  }

  .cta-row {
    margin: 0;
  }
</style>
